Dialogue: 0,-1:59:59.96,0:00:04.00,Cap,,0,0,0,,Two days after this speech,\Nthe FBI marked its speaker as a threat.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:04.58,0:00:07.71,Cap,,0,0,0,,This is the story of the speech,\Nand of the memo it caused.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.26,0:00:22.20,Cap,,0,0,0,,It starts in the spring of 1963,\Nin Birmingham, Alabama.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:22.65,0:00:27.73,Cap,,0,0,0,,Black residents there were protesting segregation,\Nand Martin Luther King was one of the leaders.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:28.30,0:00:32.60,Cap,,0,0,0,,That spring the city saw violence,\Nincluding a bombing at the Gaston Motel,
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.05,0:00:35.67,Cap,,0,0,0,,and a Library of Congress\Nphotograph shows the damage.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:36.18,0:00:41.31,Cap,,0,0,0,,Faced with the protests, President John F.\NKennedy decided to back a new civil rights law.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:41.86,0:00:47.10,Cap,,0,0,0,,On 11 June 1963, he went on radio\Nand television to explain why.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:47.69,0:00:50.43,Cap,,0,0,0,,That same day, National\NGuard troops had been needed
Dialogue: 0,0:00:50.45,0:00:53.51,Cap,,0,0,0,,to admit two Black students\Nto the University of Alabama.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:54.00,0:00:59.03,Cap,,0,0,0,,He told the country that a Black baby born\Nin America that day had about half the chance
Dialogue: 0,0:00:59.05,0:01:02.75,Cap,,0,0,0,,of finishing high school that a white\Nbaby born the same day had.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:03.26,0:01:04.67,Cap,,0,0,0,,Then he named the problem.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.61,0:01:08.72,Real,,0,0,0,,We are confronted primarily with a moral issue.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:09.11,0:01:11.82,Cap,,0,0,0,,Eight days later, he sent his proposal to Congress.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:12.35,0:01:13.86,Cap,,0,0,0,,The National Archives notes
Dialogue: 0,0:01:13.88,0:01:18.27,Cap,,0,0,0,,that the message stressed how segregation\Nhurt the nation's standing in the world,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:18.60,0:01:20.10,Cap,,0,0,0,,in the middle of the Cold War.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:20.63,0:01:24.93,Cap,,0,0,0,,Civil rights leaders wanted to show Washington\Nhow many people stood behind the bill.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:25.51,0:01:27.84,Cap,,0,0,0,,A. Philip Randolph, a veteran labor leader,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:27.91,0:01:31.94,Cap,,0,0,0,,had been calling for a march on Washington,\Nand in May King joined that call.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.51,0:01:36.19,Cap,,0,0,0,,Its official name was the March\Non Washington for Jobs and Freedom.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:36.76,0:01:38.89,Cap,,0,0,0,,It demanded a strong civil rights bill,
Dialogue: 0,0:01:39.24,0:01:43.42,Cap,,0,0,0,,protection of the vote, school desegregation,\Nand a federal jobs program.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:43.97,0:01:47.87,Cap,,0,0,0,,The date was 28 August, eleven\Nweeks after Kennedy's speech.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:53.53,0:02:00.72,Cap,,0,0,0,,On 28 August 1963, people poured into Washington\Nfrom all over the country, many of them by bus.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.33,0:02:04.38,Cap,,0,0,0,,The National Archives says\Nhundreds of thousands came.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:04.83,0:02:06.69,Cap,,0,0,0,,They gathered at the Lincoln Memorial.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:07.34,0:02:11.46,Cap,,0,0,0,,History.com reports that the leaders\Nchose it, rather than the Capitol,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:11.87,0:02:14.56,Cap,,0,0,0,,so members of Congress would not feel under siege.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:15.19,0:02:18.78,Cap,,0,0,0,,A film crew from the United States\NInformation Agency recorded the day,
Dialogue: 0,0:02:18.81,0:02:20.90,Cap,,0,0,0,,and the film still has its sound.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:21.49,0:02:24.25,Cap,,0,0,0,,Singing ran through the program,\Nand so did speeches.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:25.22,0:02:27.67,Real,,0,0,0,,We shall overcome
Dialogue: 0,0:02:28.02,0:02:29.67,Real,,0,0,0,,some day.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:30.43,0:02:33.66,Cap,,0,0,0,,Another speaker talked about how\NAmerica would look from abroad.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.42,0:02:37.24,Real,,0,0,0,,It's going to have its effect on the image
Dialogue: 0,0:02:37.26,0:02:40.43,Real,,0,0,0,,of our country all over the world.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:40.60,0:02:42.65,Cap,,0,0,0,,Then a speaker singled out King.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:43.45,0:02:46.53,Real,,0,0,0,,Clearly, the religious leader of this occasion
Dialogue: 0,0:02:46.55,0:02:48.30,Real,,0,0,0,,was Martin Luther King.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:48.56,0:02:51.97,Cap,,0,0,0,,A minister's prayer asked God\Nto bless the United States.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:52.95,0:02:55.53,Real,,0,0,0,,Pour out Thy benediction
Dialogue: 0,0:02:55.55,0:03:00.40,Real,,0,0,0,,upon the United States of America.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:00.66,0:03:02.53,Cap,,0,0,0,,According to John Lewis's memoir,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:02.66,0:03:06.62,Cap,,0,0,0,,his own speech was toned down that day\Nafter objections from other leaders.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:07.31,0:03:10.37,Cap,,0,0,0,,According to Clarence B. Jones,\NKing's lawyer and adviser,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:10.39,0:03:15.71,Cap,,0,0,0,,A. Philip Randolph argued that King should speak\Nlast, because most people were coming to hear him.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:16.24,0:03:20.97,Cap,,0,0,0,,Jones gave that account in a 2013 oral\Nhistory for the Library of Congress.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:21.52,0:03:24.23,Cap,,0,0,0,,When King reached the microphones\Nat the Lincoln Memorial,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:24.58,0:03:27.12,Cap,,0,0,0,,he began by reading from his prepared text.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:28.11,0:03:32.02,Real,,0,0,0,,Five score years ago,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:37.37,0:03:41.41,Cap,,0,0,0,,By his own account, King had finished\Nthat text around three thirty that morning.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:41.98,0:03:44.11,Cap,,0,0,0,,A typescript had already gone to the press.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:44.78,0:03:48.18,Cap,,0,0,0,,Jones says he drafted the first\Nsix or seven paragraphs,
Dialogue: 0,0:03:48.65,0:03:52.90,Cap,,0,0,0,,including the passage about\Na promissory note and insufficient funds.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:53.53,0:03:55.70,Cap,,0,0,0,,Here is King delivering that passage.
Dialogue: 0,0:03:56.61,0:04:00.15,Real,,0,0,0,,...a bad check, a check which has come back
Dialogue: 0,0:04:00.17,0:04:02.38,Real,,0,0,0,,marked "insufficient funds."
Dialogue: 0,0:04:02.69,0:04:07.29,Cap,,0,0,0,,Then, Jones says, the singer Mahalia\NJackson, who had performed earlier,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:07.36,0:04:09.63,Cap,,0,0,0,,called out to King to tell them about the dream.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:10.18,0:04:11.91,Cap,,0,0,0,,Here is Jones telling it himself.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:12.73,0:04:14.89,Real,,0,0,0,,She had performed earlier on the program,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:14.91,0:04:16.15,Real,,0,0,0,,"Tell them about the dream, Martin.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:16.17,0:04:17.40,Real,,0,0,0,,Tell them about the dream."
Dialogue: 0,0:04:17.64,0:04:22.41,Cap,,0,0,0,,Jones says King moved his text to the side\Nof the lectern and gripped the lectern,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:22.69,0:04:24.34,Cap,,0,0,0,,and that his whole manner changed.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:24.89,0:04:29.64,Cap,,0,0,0,,Jones says he turned to the person next to him\Nand said the crowd was about to go to church.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:30.25,0:04:33.97,Cap,,0,0,0,,King later recalled that he turned\Naside from the manuscript altogether.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:34.48,0:04:38.16,Cap,,0,0,0,,Other accounts agree that the dream\Npassage was not in the written text.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:38.73,0:04:40.64,Cap,,0,0,0,,But historians add a nuance.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:41.22,0:04:46.03,Cap,,0,0,0,,King had used the dream refrain before,\Nincluding in Detroit in June 1963.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:46.52,0:04:47.91,Cap,,0,0,0,,The words were not new to him.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:47.93,0:04:49.49,Cap,,0,0,0,,The moment was.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:50.06,0:04:51.83,Cap,,0,0,0,,This is where the speech turns.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:52.71,0:04:55.25,Real,,0,0,0,,...today and tomorrow,
Dialogue: 0,0:04:55.27,0:04:57.72,Real,,0,0,0,,I still have a dream.
Dialogue: 0,0:04:57.93,0:05:02.43,Cap,,0,0,0,,He spoke of his four children, and of a country\Nthat would judge them by their character.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:03.38,0:05:06.78,Real,,0,0,0,,...where they will not be judged\Nby the color of their skin,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:06.80,0:05:08.95,Real,,0,0,0,,but by the content of their character.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:09.22,0:05:15.34,Cap,,0,0,0,,Jones says King looked at his notes again only\Nnear the end, for the old hymn line, free at last.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:16.22,0:05:18.82,Real,,0,0,0,,Free at last, free at last,
Dialogue: 0,0:05:18.84,0:05:21.77,Real,,0,0,0,,thank God Almighty, we are free at last.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:22.03,0:05:25.10,Cap,,0,0,0,,That is about all of the speech this video can play.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:25.89,0:05:31.75,Cap,,0,0,0,,The National Archives says it is under copyright, so\Na complete recording is not something we can show.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:32.29,0:05:34.97,Cap,,0,0,0,,The free government film of the day never reaches it.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:35.39,0:05:38.62,Cap,,0,0,0,,What it does catch is King afterwards,\Ntalking to the camera.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:39.49,0:05:42.98,Real,,0,0,0,,...of the greatest, if not the greatest demonstrations
Dialogue: 0,0:05:43.27,0:05:45.58,Real,,0,0,0,,for freedom and human dignity
Dialogue: 0,0:05:50.83,0:05:55.75,Cap,,0,0,0,,After the march, King and the other leaders went\Nto the White House to meet President Kennedy.
Dialogue: 0,0:05:56.29,0:05:59.57,Cap,,0,0,0,,The next day, James Reston\Nwrote in the New York Times
Dialogue: 0,0:05:59.59,0:06:02.05,Cap,,0,0,0,,that King had touched all the themes of the day,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:02.26,0:06:03.89,Cap,,0,0,0,,only better than anybody else.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:04.56,0:06:08.31,Cap,,0,0,0,,Inside the FBI, a quiet fight\Nhad been going on for weeks.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:08.90,0:06:14.14,Cap,,0,0,0,,Hoover had already written on an FBI memo,\Nback in 1962, that King was no good.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:14.63,0:06:18.77,Cap,,0,0,0,,A Senate committee, led by Frank Church,\Nlater published the paper trail.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:19.36,0:06:20.69,Cap,,0,0,0,,In the week before the march,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:20.88,0:06:26.63,Cap,,0,0,0,,the bureau's domestic intelligence division sent\NDirector J. Edgar Hoover a sixty seven page study.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:27.18,0:06:32.25,Cap,,0,0,0,,It found that the Communist Party had mostly\Nfailed to win influence among Black Americans.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:32.80,0:06:34.17,Cap,,0,0,0,,Hoover disliked that answer.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:34.54,0:06:39.06,Cap,,0,0,0,,He wrote across it that it reminded him\Nof memos he had received about Fidel Castro,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:39.47,0:06:41.50,Cap,,0,0,0,,and that time had proved those analysts wrong.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:42.05,0:06:47.23,Cap,,0,0,0,,William Sullivan, who ran that division, later told\Nthe Senate that his men feared for their jobs,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:47.46,0:06:49.40,Cap,,0,0,0,,and wanted a new memo to get out of trouble.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:49.87,0:06:54.29,Cap,,0,0,0,,He wrote it on 30 August 1963,\Ntwo days after the speech.
Dialogue: 0,0:06:54.98,0:06:58.33,Cap,,0,0,0,,In it, Sullivan said that after King's\Npowerful demagogic speech,
Dialogue: 0,0:06:58.76,0:07:02.41,Cap,,0,0,0,,King stood head and shoulders above\Nall other Negro leaders put together
Dialogue: 0,0:07:02.45,0:07:04.36,Cap,,0,0,0,,in influencing great masses of people.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:04.87,0:07:10.53,Cap,,0,0,0,,He said the bureau must now mark King as the most\Ndangerous Negro of the future in this nation.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:11.15,0:07:13.27,Cap,,0,0,0,,That is the shock this story can prove.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:13.74,0:07:19.46,Cap,,0,0,0,,In 1975, Sullivan told the Senate that he put\Nin that memo what Hoover wanted to hear.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:20.01,0:07:23.06,Cap,,0,0,0,,Sullivan also told the committee\Nthat writing it meant a lot
Dialogue: 0,0:07:23.08,0:07:26.07,Cap,,0,0,0,,of nonsense that he and his men\Ndid not really believe in.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:26.56,0:07:29.32,Cap,,0,0,0,,They either did that, he said,\Nor they would be finished.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:29.83,0:07:32.89,Cap,,0,0,0,,Either way, the memo shows\Nhow the bureau read the speech.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:33.53,0:07:37.00,Cap,,0,0,0,,On 16 September, Sullivan asked\Nfor more aggressive coverage.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:37.41,0:07:40.26,Cap,,0,0,0,,Hoover refused, asking how\Nhe could switch so quickly,
Dialogue: 0,0:07:40.28,0:07:43.87,Cap,,0,0,0,,when only weeks before he had called\NCommunist influence ineffective.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:44.36,0:07:50.39,Cap,,0,0,0,,In October, Attorney General Robert Kennedy\Napproved FBI wiretaps on King's home and offices.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:50.94,0:07:55.88,Cap,,0,0,0,,The committee could not establish why he said\Nyes after refusing the same request in July.
Dialogue: 0,0:07:56.43,0:08:01.63,Cap,,0,0,0,,In December, senior FBI officials met\Nto plan ways to neutralize King's influence.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:02.22,0:08:05.19,Cap,,0,0,0,,Days later, Time magazine\Nnamed King Man of the Year,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:05.21,0:08:09.36,Cap,,0,0,0,,and Hoover said they had to dig deep\Nin the garbage to come up with that one.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:09.89,0:08:11.75,Cap,,0,0,0,,Meanwhile the law moved.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:12.48,0:08:17.36,Cap,,0,0,0,,President Johnson signed\Nthe Civil Rights Act on 2 July 1964.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:17.91,0:08:20.03,Cap,,0,0,0,,King won the Nobel Peace Prize that year.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:20.48,0:08:24.34,Cap,,0,0,0,,He received it in Oslo on 10 December 1964.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:24.81,0:08:29.40,Cap,,0,0,0,,In 1965, marchers walked from Selma\Nto Montgomery for the right to vote,
Dialogue: 0,0:08:29.76,0:08:32.55,Cap,,0,0,0,,and the Voting Rights Act was signed on 6 August.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:33.18,0:08:37.52,Cap,,0,0,0,,On 4 April 1968, King was shot\Ndead in Memphis, Tennessee.
Dialogue: 0,0:08:38.05,0:08:39.14,Cap,,0,0,0,,He was 39.
